The read-only half I did do is real.

**Target: EE102 (Circuit Analysis II — AC).** Picked on evidence, not taste: 37 author files use the per-option `whys` channel on their blanks — EE111 on all 88, EE121 on 93 — and EE102 uses it on **none** of its 102, every one emitting `"whys": null`.

**The headline finding.** The quiz kind has no way to explain a wrong answer *at all*, anywhere in the platform. `norm_quiz` takes one `why`; `app.js:2418` shows it whichever option was clicked. `blanks` has `whys`, `numeric` has `wrong`, `quiz` has nothing — across the 250 `quiz` keys in 47 author files. That is this track's entire mandate, and the schema cannot express it.

**A gate hole, in-track.** `no_positional_refs` exists because options are shuffled per learner. It runs on `why` and stops — so the 706 blanks carrying a `whys` array, whose explanations are written one per option *in option order*, are the field likeliest to say "the second option" and the one field the guard never looked at. I verified a fix is safe before writing it: all 16 positional-pattern hits in the author files are in `read` bodies and `derive` hints ("the last answer", meaning the previous step), none inside a `whys`.

**Four content defects**, arithmetic checked by hand: M7 q5's `why` rebuts "158 kHz", which **is not one of the four options** (all three real distractors go unexplained); M9 q2's rebuttal describes an operation that yields a *different* listed distractor and never mentions 1840 VA, the tempting one; M3 q6 says "each is 30 V and they differ by 1 V"; M5 q2 states 0.00999995 where it is 0.0099995.

**One defect I deliberately did not patch.** Of the ~30 questions with prose options, the correct one is strictly the longest in **16** — chance is about 8. But being precise, because the size matters: the heuristic **does not pass any single quiz**; the best it manages is 4 of 6 on M8, under the 70% bar. Fixing it means 48 new factual claims that no gate in this repo can check for truth, written blind with no emitter — which is how round-1 fixes introduced two of the ten defects round 2 found. It wants its own cycle with the toolchain up, after per-option `whys` land.

### Deliverables, in the scratchpad

- **`cycle3-apply.py`** — every edit asserts it matched exactly once and nothing is written until all have. Adds `whys` to `norm_quiz` (key *omitted* when absent, so only EE102.json changes and `emit.py --all` stays a drift detector); closes the blanks gate hole; renders per-option feedback in `app.js` mapped back through `shuffled[qi].order`, since `whys` is in authored order and indexing it by display position would rebut the wrong distractor and still test clean whenever the shuffle came out as identity; `aria-live` on the explanation slot; the four content fixes; and 24 authored `whys` across all six M7 questions in EE111's house style.
- **`cycle3-log-entry.md`** — the four-persona audit, ready to append verbatim.

Two caveats I won't paper over: **no gate ran**, so the pre-existing numbers (80 exercises / 340 checks, 21 tune units, 1091 derivation steps) are unconfirmed for this cycle rather than confirmed unchanged — though nothing was written, so they cannot have moved. And the patch script is itself **syntax-unverified**, since `py_compile` was denied; I checked its string delimiters and raw-string boundaries by hand, and I corrected one anchor that would have silently failed to match — `emit.py` writes its em dashes as literal `\u2014` escapes, not as the character.
